**If a property cannot be checked with the actions the fixture provides, do not fake it.** An assertion that holds
whatever the program does (re-reading a field nothing can change, `x >= 0` on a `u64`, a limit set so high nothing
reaches it) is worse than nothing: it reports a property as covered when it is not. Write the honest thing instead — a
`// UNCOVERABLE: <property title> — needs <the action or actor the fixture lacks>` comment, and check what you *can*.

**A defect you can see in the source but cannot observe from state is a FINDING, not a gap.** Sometimes you read the
program and the bug is plainly there — the permission check that exists on the sibling handler is absent here, a
caller-supplied account is passed where the handler should have pinned its own — and yet no sequence of actions leaves
a trace an assertion could read, because the operation *succeeds* and the damage is that it was allowed. Do not
`record_skip` that: a skip is filed where a reader looks for coverage gaps, and this is the opposite of a gap. Publish
it instead:

- declare its check as usual, and put a `// FINDING: <property title> — <what the source shows>; not observable
  from post-action state because <why>` comment in `invariants` in place of the assertion you cannot write;
- mark it `expect_check_failure`, and make the reason name the **source evidence** — the file, the function, the call
  that is missing or the argument that is unpinned — and say outright that the fuzzer cannot reproduce it.

The report then carries it as a finding resting on your reading, which is exactly what it is. Two rules keep this
honest, and it is worthless without them: never use it for a suspicion you have not confirmed in the source, and never
use it for a property you simply found hard to check — that is `// UNCOVERABLE:`, or a skip.
